BEGIN;
CREATE TEMP TABLE t (a int, UNIQUE (a) DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED);
INSERT INTO t VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO t VALUES (1);
SELECT count(*) FROM t;

The key here is "UNIQUE (a) DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED". This means the unique constraint is deferrable and initially deferred, so it's only checked at the end of the transaction (at COMMIT), not immediately after each statement.

So:
1. BEGIN - start transaction
2. CREATE TEMP TABLE - creates table with a deferrable unique constraint
3. INSERT INTO t VALUES (1) - first row, no violation yet (or even if checked, it's fine)
4. INSERT INTO t VALUES (1) - second row, duplicate, BUT the constraint is DEFERRED, so no violation is raised yet
5. SELECT count(*) FROM t - this will return 2

The answer to SELECT count(*) is 2.

